WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.750
(garage opening)

00:00:04.867 --> 00:00:07.405
(soft music)

00:00:07.405 --> 00:00:09.185
- [Brad] I don't wanna
take a shot at pilots

00:00:09.185 --> 00:00:10.707
anywhere else in the world,

00:00:10.707 --> 00:00:13.777
but adventure means different
things to different people.

00:00:13.777 --> 00:00:16.655
(garage opening)

00:00:16.655 --> 00:00:18.329
- [Jessa] I think the role
of the artist is to try

00:00:18.329 --> 00:00:21.153
and tell the story that lights your fire,

00:00:21.153 --> 00:00:23.460
and if you can do that,
then people are going to

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:28.460
pick up on that energy because
you can't help contain it.

00:00:28.561 --> 00:00:30.013
- [Brad] When Coors Light
introduced me to Jessa

00:00:30.013 --> 00:00:33.432
and she wanted to paint
something in the environment,

00:00:33.432 --> 00:00:36.906
I instantly thought of
Little Ring Mountain

00:00:36.906 --> 00:00:39.026
because there's no way to climb it,

00:00:39.026 --> 00:00:42.478
and the only way to get
here is by helicopter.

00:00:42.478 --> 00:00:45.061
(wind blowing)

00:00:48.433 --> 00:00:49.784
(paws scratching the floor)

00:00:49.784 --> 00:00:51.064
- Bentley, come on.

00:00:51.064 --> 00:00:53.001
I'm Brad Friesen.
I'm a helicopter pilot

00:00:53.001 --> 00:00:54.883
on the west coast of
British Columbia in Canada;

00:00:54.883 --> 00:00:57.604
and this is my copilot,
Master Bentley the dog.

00:00:57.604 --> 00:01:00.987
He comes with me on pretty
much all my adventures.

00:01:00.987 --> 00:01:03.829
Do you wanna go in the helicopter?

00:01:03.829 --> 00:01:05.252
Speak!

00:01:05.252 --> 00:01:06.240
(dog barking)

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.972
(upbeat music)

00:01:08.972 --> 00:01:11.505
I fly helicopters to
be able to take people

00:01:11.505 --> 00:01:14.160
and show them my backyard.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.371
(upbeat music)

00:01:16.371 --> 00:01:17.224
(Brad laughing)

00:01:17.224 --> 00:01:19.043
- [Man] Dude, that was sick!

00:01:19.043 --> 00:01:21.126
Hey, does that look real?

00:01:22.863 --> 00:01:26.613
(helicopter blades whirring)

00:01:29.523 --> 00:01:30.524
- My name's Jessa Gilbert.

00:01:30.524 --> 00:01:33.147
I'm an artist based in Squamish, B.C.

00:01:33.147 --> 00:01:35.629
I had this dream to be
able to take a big canvas

00:01:35.629 --> 00:01:38.893
out to the back country, so
that I can create an artwork,

00:01:38.893 --> 00:01:41.557
and then translate that
experience later on,

00:01:41.557 --> 00:01:44.474
hopefully, into a mural in Squamish.

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:48.972
When I first started
painting, I thought that art

00:01:48.972 --> 00:01:51.694
and adventuring had to exist in two silos,

00:01:51.694 --> 00:01:53.638
so I would go and I
would work in the studio,

00:01:53.638 --> 00:01:55.596
and then I would put that
aside, and then I would go

00:01:55.596 --> 00:01:57.741
and snowboard and explore.

00:01:57.741 --> 00:02:01.369
Moving to B.C. allowed me to
meld the two silos together

00:02:01.369 --> 00:02:05.577
and to create just one uniformed identity.

00:02:05.577 --> 00:02:08.340
I'm so excited to share
something that I find

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:12.278
so incredibly impactful
with the community.

00:02:12.278 --> 00:02:13.659
I'm really nervous about it.

00:02:13.659 --> 00:02:15.201
I hope I can do a good job.

00:02:15.201 --> 00:02:17.006
(trunk closing)

00:02:17.006 --> 00:02:18.208
(soft music)

00:02:18.208 --> 00:02:20.957
- I have never worked with an artist.

00:02:20.957 --> 00:02:22.719
I can say that.

00:02:22.719 --> 00:02:24.772
(Brad laughing)

00:02:24.772 --> 00:02:26.365
Especially not in the back country.

00:02:26.365 --> 00:02:27.703
Not even in the fore country.

00:02:27.703 --> 00:02:29.703
Is that a front country?

00:02:31.029 --> 00:02:34.779
(helicopter blades whirring)

00:02:39.488 --> 00:02:40.321
- [Brad] Morning!

00:02:40.321 --> 00:02:41.823
- [Jessa] Morning!

00:02:41.823 --> 00:02:43.119
How was your flight?

00:02:43.119 --> 00:02:45.808
- It was good once we got the
fuel truck out of the way,

00:02:45.808 --> 00:02:47.089
it was awesome.

00:02:47.089 --> 00:02:48.329
(dog panting)

00:02:48.329 --> 00:02:50.310
OK, so, uh.

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:52.830
We're gonna follow the Squamish River,

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:56.126
and then this will be
Little Ring right there.

00:02:56.126 --> 00:02:58.275
- It's so cool to get and
to look at more the tops

00:02:58.275 --> 00:03:00.308
of the peaks, rather than
seeing it from the valley.

00:03:00.308 --> 00:03:01.167
- Yeah, I'm so stoked, yeah.

00:03:01.167 --> 00:03:03.139
Instead of this, you'll be doing that.

00:03:03.139 --> 00:03:04.802
And it's not climbable; it's not something

00:03:04.802 --> 00:03:07.856
that people can get to
without a helicopter.

00:03:07.856 --> 00:03:08.933
- Even cooler.

00:03:08.933 --> 00:03:10.168
You're gonna get us there!

00:03:10.168 --> 00:03:11.001
- Yeah. Alright

00:03:11.001 --> 00:03:12.857
(helicopter blades whirring)

00:03:12.857 --> 00:03:14.501
Hey, it's Squamish traffic R44, helicopter

00:03:14.501 --> 00:03:17.135
Golf Yankee Yankee Whiskey plus one.

00:03:17.135 --> 00:03:21.546
Northbound up the Squamish
River. Any conflicting? Golf Yankee Yankee Whiskey

00:03:21.546 --> 00:03:23.529
- [Man] K, copy that, thanks.

00:03:23.529 --> 00:03:26.092
(Jessa laughing)

00:03:26.092 --> 00:03:27.826
- [Jessa] That's crazy.

00:03:27.826 --> 00:03:30.463
- [Brad] Heading Northbound at 1,500 feet.

00:03:30.463 --> 00:03:31.705
- We're goin' right in it.

00:03:31.705 --> 00:03:34.370
- Oh, yeah, we're going right into it.

00:03:34.370 --> 00:03:38.120
(helicopter blades whirring)

00:03:40.282 --> 00:03:42.699
(soft music)

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:49.443
- Ring and then lower.

00:03:49.443 --> 00:03:51.109
(soft music)

00:03:51.109 --> 00:03:54.609
- Wow, they really stand out in the range.

00:03:57.302 --> 00:04:01.052
(helicopter blades whirring)

00:04:09.314 --> 00:04:10.731
- [Brad] Bentley!

00:04:15.964 --> 00:04:17.907
- It's incredibly humbling being up here

00:04:17.907 --> 00:04:19.713
because you realize how small you are

00:04:19.713 --> 00:04:21.788
in the grand scheme of things.

00:04:21.788 --> 00:04:25.220
You don't even make a blip in the radar.

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:27.647
I wanted to see the
terrain that was around

00:04:27.647 --> 00:04:30.809
to get the best orientation
and the best composition.

00:04:30.809 --> 00:04:35.103
The problem was that
everything is stunning.

00:04:35.103 --> 00:04:37.679
I did four or five
sketches and it was like

00:04:37.679 --> 00:04:40.811
I don't know how I'm
actually gonna choose this.

00:04:40.811 --> 00:04:45.252
Every time I walked around
the peak, you get another view

00:04:45.252 --> 00:04:48.502
that has something incredible about it.

00:04:49.625 --> 00:04:52.857
(reverent music)

00:04:52.857 --> 00:04:55.319
That one stuck out to me because
not only is it the texture

00:04:55.319 --> 00:04:57.062
of the landscape, but it tells the story

00:04:57.062 --> 00:04:59.238
of how we arrived here.

00:04:59.238 --> 00:05:01.168
It shows the way in which
we navigated through

00:05:01.168 --> 00:05:03.718
and also the similarity
in the landscape that

00:05:03.718 --> 00:05:06.218
we're experiencing right here.

00:05:09.633 --> 00:05:12.124
Do you mind helping me set up my studio?

00:05:12.124 --> 00:05:13.488
- I like helping.

00:05:13.488 --> 00:05:17.206
(Brad and Jessa laughing)

00:05:17.206 --> 00:05:18.339
- [Brad] Wow!

00:05:18.339 --> 00:05:19.696
- This is definitely the most back-country

00:05:19.696 --> 00:05:21.613
my easel has ever been.

00:05:22.558 --> 00:05:25.231
(supplies unrolling)

00:05:25.231 --> 00:05:28.049
(pen brushing across canvas)

00:05:28.049 --> 00:05:30.107
The technique that I
use for these creations is

00:05:30.107 --> 00:05:31.755
called a single line.

00:05:31.755 --> 00:05:34.754
The goal is that once the pen
touches down on the panel,

00:05:34.754 --> 00:05:36.254
it never comes up.

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:39.843
(wind blowing)

00:05:40.897 --> 00:05:42.759
The reason why I like this
technique is that it requires

00:05:42.759 --> 00:05:46.664
that you're fully engaged in
your practice and your process

00:05:46.664 --> 00:05:50.497
the entire time you're
creating the landscape.

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:54.897
(footsteps)

00:05:54.897 --> 00:05:56.052
- Whoa. This is awesome.

00:05:56.052 --> 00:05:56.885
- Thanks!

00:05:58.302 --> 00:06:00.274
- It's really cool.

00:06:00.274 --> 00:06:03.333
Temperature's dropping,
how are you feeling that?

00:06:03.333 --> 00:06:07.679
- Uh, probably wrap up
once the sun goes down.

00:06:07.679 --> 00:06:09.755
- Sun's gone down; I dunno
if you noticed or not.

00:06:09.755 --> 00:06:11.120
- Well, once that disappears.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:13.090
(Brad and Jessa laughing)

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:14.898
(wind blowing)

00:06:14.898 --> 00:06:17.845
(footsteps crunching)

00:06:17.845 --> 00:06:20.343
- What time do you think
you're gonna get up and start?

00:06:20.343 --> 00:06:21.611
- I'd love to be up for sunrise.

00:06:21.611 --> 00:06:23.079
- Whoa, cool.

00:06:23.079 --> 00:06:26.705
-You have fun.
(Brad and Jessa laughing)

00:06:26.705 --> 00:06:28.843
(wind blowing)

00:06:28.843 --> 00:06:31.593
(peaceful music)

00:06:40.025 --> 00:06:42.899
(equipment clinking against rocks)

00:06:42.899 --> 00:06:45.649
(liquid pouring)

00:06:53.105 --> 00:06:56.188
(footsteps on rocks)

00:07:06.486 --> 00:07:09.706
(fingers sliding on canvas)

00:07:09.706 --> 00:07:12.131
- There's always this tension about coming

00:07:12.131 --> 00:07:14.441
back to the painting the
next day after you leave it

00:07:14.441 --> 00:07:18.978
the night before about what
does it actually look like.

00:07:18.978 --> 00:07:21.311
Is it capturing that moment?

00:07:22.146 --> 00:07:24.077
What I wanna capture is
really just how it feels

00:07:24.077 --> 00:07:26.983
to spend time outside
and feel the landscape

00:07:26.983 --> 00:07:28.624
and feel connected to it.

00:07:28.624 --> 00:07:31.239
Hopefully I don't screw it up.

00:07:31.239 --> 00:07:33.489
(laughing)

00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:38.958
I hope that the painting that I
create can really showcase

00:07:38.958 --> 00:07:41.347
and celebrate what it
feels like to be up here

00:07:41.347 --> 00:07:43.347
because it's incredible.

00:07:45.282 --> 00:07:48.557
- Just watching Jessa telling
the story of where we are,

00:07:48.557 --> 00:07:52.222
what she's doing here, is
gonna translate incredibly well

00:07:52.222 --> 00:07:53.805
to the final mural.

00:07:54.897 --> 00:07:56.179
I think it's gonna have a lot of meaning

00:07:56.179 --> 00:07:58.248
for a lot of people.

00:07:58.248 --> 00:07:59.599
(upbeat music)

00:07:59.599 --> 00:08:00.971
- I hope to do this again.

00:08:00.971 --> 00:08:02.721
I'm so -- I'm filled.

00:08:03.954 --> 00:08:05.037
It's amazing.

00:08:07.144 --> 00:08:09.727
(upbeat music)

00:08:11.337 --> 00:08:13.920
(wind blowing)

00:08:18.793 --> 00:08:20.126
This is a beast.

00:08:21.326 --> 00:08:23.821
The larger the wall, the bigger the risk,

00:08:23.821 --> 00:08:27.654
and the bigger impact
it can potentially make.

00:08:30.082 --> 00:08:32.267
I wanna make sure that
I'm giving the community

00:08:32.267 --> 00:08:35.627
something that they can be proud of.

00:08:35.627 --> 00:08:39.127
(spray paint can shaking)

00:08:42.048 --> 00:08:44.715
(calming music)

00:08:47.923 --> 00:08:50.506
(wind blowing)

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:58.231
Tomorrow morning, early, I
have some helpers coming.

00:08:58.231 --> 00:09:02.537
I'm still nervous about how
I'm going to tell people

00:09:02.537 --> 00:09:05.883
what I need and that I
don't totally blow it

00:09:05.883 --> 00:09:08.141
'cause I got a lot of work to do.

00:09:08.141 --> 00:09:10.724
(upbeat music)

00:09:27.304 --> 00:09:31.289
This is starting to look really good, guys.

00:09:31.289 --> 00:09:32.956
- We're almost here.

00:09:34.321 --> 00:09:35.901
Is this is it?

00:09:35.901 --> 00:09:37.234
You ready? Jump!

00:09:38.331 --> 00:09:39.698
- Hi, how's it going?

00:09:39.698 --> 00:09:40.804
How've you been?

00:09:40.804 --> 00:09:42.007
- Good.

00:09:42.007 --> 00:09:43.171
This is it?

00:09:43.171 --> 00:09:44.497
- This is it. Yeah.

00:09:44.497 --> 00:09:45.698
Plugging away, day two.

00:09:45.698 --> 00:09:47.459
- Wait, so, I don't
remember palm trees on top

00:09:47.459 --> 00:09:48.438
of a mountain.

00:09:48.438 --> 00:09:49.971
- They're not palm trees!

00:09:49.971 --> 00:09:52.009
They're ferns, Brad.

00:09:52.009 --> 00:09:54.592
(cars driving)

00:09:57.811 --> 00:10:01.067
Today was a really productive day.

00:10:01.067 --> 00:10:04.499
I go into what I call
the creative blackout.

00:10:04.499 --> 00:10:08.915
I lose track of time, I
suddenly kinda wake up

00:10:08.915 --> 00:10:11.390
a couple hours later.

00:10:11.390 --> 00:10:14.140
(peaceful music)

00:10:16.017 --> 00:10:18.916
I'm thinking a lot about
what Squamish means.

00:10:18.916 --> 00:10:22.744
Depending on what First Nation
you ask, it means either

00:10:22.744 --> 00:10:24.994
Mother Wind or Strong Wind.

00:10:26.145 --> 00:10:27.955
The reason that the
clouds are so playful here

00:10:27.955 --> 00:10:31.038
is because of that wind, that energy.

00:10:32.748 --> 00:10:35.024
I wanna make sure that that
wind gets visually articulated

00:10:35.024 --> 00:10:36.774
in the piece somehow.

00:10:39.004 --> 00:10:39.837
We'll see.

00:10:39.837 --> 00:10:42.651
(chuckling)

00:10:42.651 --> 00:10:45.234
(wind blowing)

00:10:46.259 --> 00:10:48.676
(soft music)

00:10:57.733 --> 00:11:00.483
(people talking)

00:11:06.588 --> 00:11:08.850
I got fully in my headphones
and when I turned around,

00:11:08.850 --> 00:11:10.699
there was like fifty people here.

00:11:10.699 --> 00:11:12.943
(laughing)

00:11:12.943 --> 00:11:14.610
- Hey, how ya doing?

00:11:16.261 --> 00:11:19.594
I've never had anybody interpret a ride.

00:11:20.467 --> 00:11:22.867
She's taken sort of the essence of flying,

00:11:22.867 --> 00:11:25.818
and she's translated the whole
trip from not only the colors

00:11:25.818 --> 00:11:28.370
but the feeling of getting
to the environment.

00:11:28.370 --> 00:11:30.938
(Jessa laughing)

00:11:30.938 --> 00:11:34.163
- I wanna be able to share
the joy and the gratitude

00:11:34.163 --> 00:11:38.015
I have for these places with
as many people as possible.

00:11:38.015 --> 00:11:40.091
That's what's kinda nice
about the mountains is

00:11:40.091 --> 00:11:41.662
nobody owns them.

00:11:41.662 --> 00:11:43.047
Thank you so much for coming out.

00:11:43.047 --> 00:11:44.964
This is -- I might cry.

00:11:45.851 --> 00:11:47.889
(laughing)

00:11:47.889 --> 00:11:50.002
Ah, this is a really big moment.

00:11:50.002 --> 00:11:52.153
There are so many dreams that came true

00:11:52.153 --> 00:11:53.317
with this whole project.

00:11:53.317 --> 00:11:56.537
I got to make a public piece
of art in the town that I love,

00:11:56.537 --> 00:11:58.069
that I live in.

00:11:58.069 --> 00:12:00.902
(people cheering)

00:12:04.496 --> 00:12:07.839
- Everybody, cheers to
Jessa, this amazing artwork,

00:12:07.839 --> 00:12:10.681
and this great town that inspired it.

00:12:10.681 --> 00:12:13.690
(people cheering)

00:12:13.690 --> 00:12:16.307
- This landscape changed me
into a better version of myself,

00:12:16.307 --> 00:12:18.761
and I hope that the artwork
that I make and this mural

00:12:18.761 --> 00:12:21.233
can help inspire other
people to get outside

00:12:21.233 --> 00:12:25.130
and perhaps get on their own adventures.

00:12:25.130 --> 00:12:27.713
(upbeat music)

00:12:36.903 --> 00:12:40.153
(toned bell sounding)

